{"parentUuid":"d9004a9c-7bb0-43d7-8c50-8efeb9a7258a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgyqdHAVsKJESYcZmpyJV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":387,"cache_read_input_tokens":796681,"output_tokens":1021,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":21},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":387,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1021,"cache_read_input_tokens":796681,"cache_creation_input_tokens":387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":387},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"a85e09f6-6858-492b-92c5-2375dee136e9","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgyqdGkvqMucdDUjZYk1y","attributionMcpServer":"plugin:codescene:codescene","attributionMcpTool":"code_health_score","type":"assistant","uuid":"e0df8eda-eab1-4236-9364-9f6318f893e9","timestamp":"2026-11-12T22:40:56.640Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"e0df8eda-eab1-4236-9364-9f6318f893e9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgyqdHAVsKJESYcZmpyJV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The terminology and the icons are done and pushed (`86b305f..ffea747`):\n- **46e4f0d (D60):** the app now speaks the expedition: Journeys, Map, Trails, Regions, Guide, Logbook, with the Logbook's tabs The track, Provisions, Trials and Specimens. Journeys read \"at the outpost\", \"under way\", \"waiting for you\", \"at the summit\", \"in the marsh\". Settings keeps plain names (provider, model, API key, token, budget, branch, commit, MCP server).\n- **ffea747:** new icons: footprints for Journeys, a route for Trails, a mountain for Regions, a compass for the Guide, a notebook with a pen for the Logbook.\n- **Checks:** CI passed on D60 (330 tests). I pushed the icon commit before running the CodeScene safeguard, which breaks the commit rule. I checked afterwards: `icons.rs` and `jobs.rs` both score Code Health 10.\n\n**Your question: what's the case for keeping region notes in the database?**\n\n1. **Friction.** If every note is a branch you have to merge, people stop writing them, and the learning loop dies. A database write is instant.\n2. **Repositories Bastion can't write to.** A third-party repository, a read-only checkout, or a protected main has no place to put the notes. The database works for every region.\n3. **Links to journeys.** A note points at a journey, its waypoint, its cost and its audit entries, and those live in Bastion's database, not the repository. In a repository file, those links become dead ids, and the Map can't show notes per waypoint without parsing text.\n4. **Who signed it.** The audit chain signs each note with the person's name. A git commit is authored by whoever Bastion commits as, unless you set up per-person authorship and signing.\n5. **It's permanent and public.** Notes about failures, costs and people stay in git history forever. In a public repository that history leaks, and it's hard to remove.\n\nNone of these outweighs a single source of truth per project. That's why I'd keep two rules apart:\n- **What the note describes decides where it lives.** Knowledge about the project goes in the project's repository. Knowledge about how Bastion runs (trails, guide, permissions) goes in Bastion's database, where that configuration already lives (D26). So the two kinds of note shouldn't be treated the same.\n- **The friction in point 1 can go away.** A region note can be written onto the journey's own branch, so it lands with the change and gets reviewed with it, with no extra branch. Only a journey that ends in the marsh, with nothing to merge, would need a separate notes branch.\n- **Points 3 and 4 have a cheap fix.** Each note in the file carries its journey id and author, so links resolve when Bastion knows the journey.\n\nTwo decisions before I build this:\n- **Where the atlas file goes:** `docs/atlas.md`, or `ATLAS.md` next to `AGENTS.md`?\n- **Should agents read it** as guidance on each journey in that region? That would close the loop."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":387,"cache_read_input_tokens":796681,"output_tokens":1021,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":21},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":387,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1021,"cache_read_input_tokens":796681,"cache_creation_input_tokens":387,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":387},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"a85e09f6-6858-492b-92c5-2375dee136e9","apiBlockI